  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "⚔️ Half-Orc Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names that depend on who raised them: Bram Ironside, Ugak Bonebreaker, or Thalric, somewhere in between."
    },
    "intro": "A half-orc's name says more about who raised them than about their blood. One brought up in a human village might be plain Bram or Hester; one raised in an orc camp, Ugak or Mogsha; and one who's never quite belonged to either might have a name that's a bit of both. This **half-orc name generator** lets you choose the upbringing, then adds a surname or a deed-name to match.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Names follow upbringing",
        "content": "<p>The D&amp;D Player's Handbook has always said this: half-orcs usually have names from the culture they were raised in. A half-orc raised by humans takes a human name; one raised by orcs, an orc name. Some choose the other kind on purpose, to fit in or to make a point.</p><ul><li><strong>Among humans</strong>: short, plain, working names, the kind given in farming villages and frontier towns. The surname is a trade or a place.</li><li><strong>Among orcs</strong>: hard orc names, with a deed-name that has to be earned.</li><li><strong>Between two worlds</strong>: names with orc sounds and human shapes, for a half-orc who has drifted between both.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "A name with a history",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Two names at once.</strong> A half-orc might have an orc name from their mother's people and a human name for town. Which one they give says who they think they're talking to.</li><li><strong>A name they chose.</strong> Plenty of half-orcs in fiction drop the name they were given. That's a good backstory question: who gave you your name, and why don't you use it?</li><li><strong>A deed-name in a human town.</strong> <em>Skullsplitter</em> means one thing in an orc camp and another on a wanted poster.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Half-orcs in the 2024 rules",
        "content": "<p>The 2024 Player's Handbook dropped half-orcs as a separate choice: an orc character with human family is now just an orc, or a human, with the background you give them. The names work exactly the same way, and this generator fits either edition.</p><p>For full orcs, try the <a href=\"orc-name-generator.html\">Orc Name Generator</a>, and for a half-orc who took up a sword and a code, the <a href=\"knight-name-generator.html\">Knight Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Should a half-orc have an orc name or a human name?",
        "answer": "Whichever the people who raised them would give. Choose \"Among humans\" or \"Among orcs\" for that, or \"Between two worlds\" for a name that mixes both sounds."
      },
      {
        "question": "What's a deed-name?",
        "answer": "An orc-style surname that describes something the character did, like Bonebreaker or Elfbiter. In orc culture it's earned, and it can be lost."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names in D&D?",
        "answer": "Yes, they're free to use and you don't need to credit the site. They suit half-orcs from the 2014 rules and orc characters with human family from the 2024 rules."
      }
    ]
  }
